---
description: Every user-visible string comes from the catalog, never from a literal
globs: src/**/*.cpp
alwaysApply: false
---

# No user-visible literals outside src/strings/

```cpp
// ❌ BAD
return ui::panel("Description", body, false);
ctx_.set_status("Loading " + key + "…");

// ✅ GOOD
return ui::panel(tr("ticket.description"), body, false);
ctx_.set_status(tr("ticket.message.loading", {{"key", key}}));
```

`using translations::tr;` goes next to `using namespace ftxui;` at the top of
the file, so call sites stay short.

A literal works perfectly and is invisible to translation, so this is the one
rule here that nothing will catch for you.

# Composing sentences

Pass the whole sentence to the catalog with `{placeholders}`, and let the
translation decide the word order. Never concatenate a translated fragment with
punctuation or another fragment:

```cpp
// ❌ BAD — a translator cannot reorder this, and the comma may not belong
tr("project.status.loaded") + ", " + std::to_string(count)

// ✅ GOOD
tr("project.status.tabLoaded", {{"project", key}, {"tab", tab}, {"count", n}})
```

Where English picks between two phrasings, give each one its own key
(`tabLoaded` / `tabLoadedPartial`) rather than splicing a suffix in.

# Keys

Dotted path into the tree in `src/strings/en.cpp`:
`<window or area>.<thing>[.<variant>]`. A node holds its own text under `"_"`
and children beside it, so `ticket.assignee` is the label and
`ticket.assignee.unassigned` the placeholder shown in it. Ordered lists are JSON
arrays read with `translations::tr_list`.

An unknown key renders as `!the.key!` and an unfilled placeholder stays as
`{name}`, so both show up on screen and in the smoke recordings.

# Not strings

Leave these as literals, and do not add keys for them:

- key chords (`"j / k"`, `"Enter / Space"`) — bindings, not prose. Their
  descriptions are translated; the keys are not.
- JQL, field names, API paths, and anything else Jira parses.
- Jira's own data: status names, issue types, response bodies. `status_color`
  matches on English status names from the API on purpose.
